  "oracle_attribution": "The test directly exercises the new PRNG driver interface and its sfc32/sfc16 implementations, which the PR's stated purpose is to introduce (it consolidates random.c around a single PRNG and adds sfc16/sfc32 of PractRand as the default non-cryptographic PRNG). At the base, os/lib/random.h defines only random_init/random_rand and contains no struct random_prng nor sfc32_prng/sfc16_prng (confirmed by compile_requirements: both absent at base), and os/lib has no sfc32-prng.c/sfc16-prng.c, so the new native unit cannot compile under WERROR=1. The build break is the missing interface the PR adds, not an incidental one. After the PR, the sfc32/sfc16 implementations are compiled into the native binary (os/lib is added as a MODULE) and the PractRand conformance checks pass.",
